<Page
  title="The “honored through” view"
  lede="Stop the clock in a chosen year and see what had already been promised."
>
  <p>
    Choose a year. Land the federal record shows leaving Native hands on or before that year under a
    ratified treaty or an agreement (a recorded cession) is drawn in grey, with any documented
    consent problems still hatched. Land taken by then by an Act of Congress, an executive order, a
    treaty the Senate never ratified, or with no instrument at all keeps its own color: no cession
    is on the record for it, so the view never counts it as given. Land the record shows lost only
    later is drawn in green: under the instruments in force that year, it would still have been held
    by the nations. As you move the slider past the chosen year, each later loss appears as an
    outline, so you can see what changed and when.
  </p>
  <p>The choice of years is fixed so the view is not cherry-picked:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>1783</strong>: the end of the Revolutionary War, before any federal treaty.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>1830</strong>: the Indian Removal Act.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>1851</strong>: the first Fort Laramie treaty.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>1868</strong>: the second Fort Laramie treaty.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>1871</strong>: Congress ends treaty-making.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>1887</strong>: the General Allotment Act.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <h2>What this view does not do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Grey does not mean the land was given freely or lawfully. It means only that the United States
      recorded an instrument for it. Many recorded cessions were signed under duress, by contested
      signers or with nations left out; open an area for what is documented and how well it is
      sourced.
    </li>
    <li>
      It does not show land Royce never mapped: colonial and state takings before 1784 or outside
      federal treaty-making, most of Texas, Alaska and Hawaii, or anything after 1894.
    </li>
    <li>
      It is not a legal claim. A “Legal status today” panel always accompanies it, separating what a
      treaty said from what courts and Congress have done since.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    This view and its legal-status panel are awaiting review by a federal Indian law reviewer.{' '}
    <a href={href('/map/#v=honored&cut=1851&y=1868')}>Try it on the map</a>.
  </p>
</Page>
